              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 3.7: Internal structure of a sunflower stem"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.479,
            0.084,
            0.89,
            0.238
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "might be the reason for the presence of different types of cells and tissues? You can see that different groups of cells are present in the T.S. of sunflower stem and each tissue is specialised to perform a specific function. These are "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "permanent tissues",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Permanent tissues can be simple (composed of only one type of cell) or complex (composed of more than one types of cells)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.479,
            0.241,
            0.889,
            0.277
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Observe the types of tissues given in Fig. 3.7 based on pointers provided as follows:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.481,
            0.28,
            0.805,
            0.297
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i)\t Protective tissue — Epidermis"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.479,
            0.3,
            0.888,
            0.352
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What protects plants from mechanical injury, water loss, harmful microorganisms and extreme environmental conditions?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.478,
            0.354,
            0.89,
            0.506
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "epidermis",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " forms the outermost layer of the plant body. It consists of a tightly packed, single layer of flat and rectangular cells. It protects all parts of the plants. These cells are covered with a waxy layer of cutin called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "cuticle",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". In some plants, living in very dry habitat, the epidemis may be covered by a thick layer of cuticle to reduce the water loss in the process of transpiration by"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.047,
            0.516,
            0.214,
            0.617
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 7,
              "bbox": [
                0.047,
                0.516,
                0.214,
                0.617
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_4_image_7.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_footnote",
              "index": 8,
              "bbox": [
                0.076,
                0.617,
                0.192,
                0.631
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "(a) Parenchyma"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.506,
            0.892,
            0.661
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "stomata. The cuticle also provides protection against mechenical injury and invasion by prasites. In many plants, hair-like projections arise from epidermal cells. In roots, these projections are called root hair, which increase the surface area for absorption of water and minerals from the soil. In leaves, the epidermis contains pores called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "stomata",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", which apart from gaseous exchange helps in transpiration, i.e., evaporation of water vapours through stomata. Thus, transpiration helps in water transportation by creating a transpiration pull in xylem. Transpiration also helps in elimination of wastes from the plant body."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.048,
            0.652,
            0.213,
            0.748
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 10,
              "bbox": [
                0.048,
                0.652,
                0.213,
                0.748
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_4_image_10.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_footnote",
              "index": 11,
              "bbox": [
                0.07,
                0.749,
                0.19,
                0.763
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "(b) Collenchyma"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "image_footnote",
              "index": 12,
              "bbox": [
                0.064,
                0.774,
                0.208,
                0.789
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Thick lignified walls"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.047,
            0.792,
            0.214,
            0.879
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 13,
              "bbox": [
                0.047,
                0.792,
                0.214,
                0.879
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_4_image_13.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_footnote",
              "index": 14,
              "bbox": [
                0.072,
                0.88,
                0.197,
                0.893
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "(c) Sclerenchyma"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "image_footnote",
              "index": 15,
              "bbox": [
                0.039,
                0.895,
                0.222,
                0.923
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 3.8: Various types of simple permanent tissues"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.683,
            0.892,
            0.771
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What keeps a plant upright? Why does a fresh twig bend but a dry twig break? Why are seed coats hard and how do aquatic plants float? These functions are carried out by supporting tissues. There are three types of supporting tissues or simple permanent tissues — parenchyma, collenchyma and sclerenchyma. Each differs in structure and performs supporting functions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.663,
            0.705,
            0.681
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii)\t Supporting tissue — Simple permanent tissues"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.774,
            0.37,
            0.791
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "a. Parenchyma"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.793,
            0.892,
            0.865
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Parenchyma consists of living cells with thin walls (Fig. 3.8a). These cells are loosely packed with intercellular spaces. Parenchyma mainly stores food but also performs photosynthesis in the green parts of the plants. In aquatic plants, specialised parenchyma forms air spaces, which help them float."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.866,
            0.374,
            0.883
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "b.Collenchyma"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.886,
            0.891,
            0.923
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Collenchyma consists of living cells with unevenly thickened corners due to pectin (a chemical that gives flexibility like rubber) deposition (Fig. 3.8b)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
